{"type":"process","title":"Access governance, VPC isolation, and audit logging","lead":"Zero public IPs, VPC Service Controls, and complete API audit logging enforce HIPAA security.","center":"ePHI SQL Server perimeter","items":[{"title":"Zero public IP addresses","text":"SQL Server instances use private RFC 1918 addresses in the Shared VPC, eliminating direct public internet exposure.","icon":"triangle alert"},{"title":"IAP tunnel RDP administration","text":"Admins reach Windows nodes via IAP, removing external bastions.","icon":"security"},{"title":"VPC Service Controls boundary","text":"VPC Service Controls protect storage and keys, blocking clinical data exfiltration beyond authorized boundary networks.","icon":"scale"},{"title":"Least-privilege IAM roles","text":"Granular IAM roles split platform tasks from internal SQL duties.","icon":"team"},{"title":"Active Directory authentication","text":"Managed Microsoft AD centralizes Kerberos authentication and enforces policies across cluster nodes.","icon":"badge check"},{"title":"Immutable Cloud Audit Logs","text":"Administrative actions and Secret Manager queries stream directly to locked log buckets for regulatory audits.","icon":"cloud"}],"governance":["All administrative and data-access API calls logged for HIPAA non-repudiation","Private Google Access restricts storage and KMS traffic to Google backbone","Role-based access separates cloud infrastructure admins from SQL DBAs"],"notes":"To meet Google Cloud HIPAA technical safeguards, all SQL Server VMs run with zero public IPs and require Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP) tunnels for administrative access. VPC Service Controls enclose the ePHI environment while Cloud Audit Logs track every administrative and data-access API call for non-repudiation.

