{"type":"cards","design":"S66_QUAD_RULE_CARDS","title":"HIPAA encryption controls for data at rest and transit","lead":"Layered encryption with CMEK and TLS 1.2 meets strict HIPAA mandates across storage and networks.","items":[{"title":"Cloud KMS customer-managed keys","text":"Contoso Health owns key rotation and revocation in Cloud KMS and Cloud HSM to protect Persistent Disks independently under the Google Cloud BAA. Dedicated cryptographic boundaries ensure complete organizational custody.","tag":"Security team","icon":"hex-lock"},{"title":"SQL Server TDE for files","text":"Transparent Data Encryption encrypts MDF, NDF, and LDF files at the database engine level before any blocks reach underlying storage volumes. Database administrators enforce cryptographic protection without application changes.","tag":"DBA team","icon":"shield-database"},{"title":"TLS 1.2 endpoint traffic","text":"Availability group replication endpoints on TCP port 5022 enforce certificate-based TLS 1.2 encryption alongside Google Cloud default IPsec and TLS 1.3 regional backbone transit.","tag":"Network team","icon":"shield-cloud"},{"title":"Encrypted Persistent Disk snapshots","text":"Regional and multi-region Persistent Disk snapshots inherit CMEK protection automatically to prevent unauthorized restoration of clinical ePHI volumes in secondary projects. Granular policies enforce strict isolation across projects.","tag":"Cloud ops","icon":"cloud-layers"}],"notes":"To satisfy HIPAA technical safeguards under the Google Cloud Business Associate Agreement, Contoso Health retains control over encryption keys via Cloud KMS and Cloud HSM. SQL Server Transparent Data Encryption works alongside CMEK-protected Persistent Disks and encrypted snapshots, while replication traffic across regions is secured with TLS 1.2 endpoints over Google Cloud's encrypted backbone.

